  "strategy": "1.Approach and rationale\nWe use a hybrid evolutionary-local search approach to solve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The core idea is to combine global exploration via genetic programming with local refinement techniques. Unlike the parent program's multi-resolution optimization, we introduce:\n- A direct parent-offspring crossover that preserves both bounds and integral constraint without iterative adjustment\n- Gaussian mutation with adaptive step size control\n- Tournament selection with exponential fitness weighting\n- Early termination if no improvement is observed\n- Repair mechanism that enforces [0,1] bounds and sum constraint simultaneously\n\nThis approach addresses limitations in the parent program's repair function (200 iterations per call) and premature convergence by combining global exploration with targeted local refinement.\n\n2.Mathematical specification\nRepresentation:\n- h(x) is represented as a NumPy array of length 560\n- Values satisfy h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i with sum(h) = 280 (since \u2211h dx = 1, dx=2/560)\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over k \u2208 [-559, 558] (full correlation mode)\n\nConstraint enforcement:\nAfter any mutation/crossover operation:\n1. Clip values to [0,1]\n2. Adjust the integral to exactly 280 by scaling the largest residuals\n\nOperations:\n- Crossover: Uniform, probability 0.95\n- Mutation: Gaussian, probability 0.05 (adaptive step size based on fitness)\n- Repair: Single-pass clipping and adjustment\n\n3.Implementation procedure\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    t0_start = time.time()\n    \n    # Parameters\n    n_points = 560\n    target_sum = 280  # n_points/2 * (2/560) dx factor is absorbed in evaluation\n    pop_size = 40\n    generations = 30\n    mutation_prob = 0.05\n    crossover_prob = 0.95\n    time_remaining = budget_s\n\n    # Repair function\n    def repair(parent):\n        h = np.clip(parent, 0.0, 1.0)\n        s = np.sum(h)\n        if abs(s - target_sum) < 1e-6:\n            return h\n        # Rapid adjustment to satisfy sum constraint\n        diff = target_sum - s\n        if diff == 0:\n            return h\n        # Create residual array\n        h_sorted = np.sort(h)\n        # Combine clipping and adjustment\n        while abs(diff) > 1e-6:\n            # Clip first 5% of invalid values\n            if diff > 0:\n                clip_values = h_sorted[:11]  # First 11 smallest values (5%)\n                new_clip = min(np.max(h), 1.0) # Avoid regressive clipping\n                diff_reduction = (new_clip - clip_values).sum() * len(clip_values) / n_points\n                diff -= diff_reduction\n                h[:11] = new_clip\n            else:\n                clip_values = h_sorted[-11:]  # Largest 11 values (5%)\n                new_clip = max(np.min(h), 0.0)\n                diff_reduction = (clip_values - new_clip).sum() * len(clip_values) / n_points\n                diff -= diff_reduction\n                h[-11:] = new_clip\n        return np.clip(h, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n    # Roll credits\n    def roll_credits(parent):\n        h = parent.copy()\n        s = np.sum(h)\n        residual = target_sum - s\n        if residual < 0:\n            # Truncate parts of the function\n            sorted_idx = np.argsort(-h)  # Desorted indices\n            h10 = h[sorted_idx][:10].mean()\n            h[h < h10] = 0.7  # Lower tail adjustment\n        elif residual > 0:\n            # Promote parts of the function\n            sorted_idx = np.argsort(h)  # Sorted indices\n            h10 = h[sorted_idx][:10].mean()\n            h[h > h10] = 0.3  # Upper tail adjustment\n        return repair(h)\n\n    # Distance-aware crossover\n    def crossover(parent1, parent2):\n        child = np.where(np.random.random(len(parent1)) < 0.5,\n                        parent1, parent2)\n        return repair(child)\n\n    # Create initial population\n    parent = initial_h_values.copy()\n    population = [parent]\n    for _ in range(pop_size-1):\n        rnd = np.random.RandomState(np.random.randint(10000))\n        candidate = np.random.random(n_points)\n        population.append(roll_credits(candidate))\n    \n    # Fitness evaluation\n    def fitness(solution):\n        corr = np.max(np.correlate(solution, 1-solution, mode='full'))\n        time_due = time.time() - t0_start\n        return -corr * (2.0/n_points) + (time_remaining/time_budget/100 if time_due < time_budget else -100)\n    \n    def evaluate_all(pop):\n        return {np.argmax(fitness(ind)): fitness(ind) for ind in pop}\n    \n    time_budget = 1100  # fixed parent budget\n    best_index, best_fitness = max(evaluate_all(population).items(), key=lambda x: x[1])\n    best_solution = population[best_index].copy()\n    \n    # Main evolution loop\n    for gen in range(generations):\n        time_start = time.time()\n        current_best = max(evaluate_all(population), key=lambda x: x[1])\n        if current_best != best_index:\n            best_index, best_fitness = current_best\n            best_solution = population[best_index]\n            if fitness(best_solution) < 0.3800:  # Lower is better\n                print(f\"Improvement to {best_fitness}\")\n        \n        # Apply mutation to top solutions\n        for i in range(pop_size):\n            if np.random.random() < mutation_prob:\n                solution = population[i]\n                mutation = np.random.normal(0, 0.01, n_points)\n                child = np.clip(solution + mutation, 0, 1)\n                child = roll_credits(child)\n                child_fitness = fitness(child)\n                population[i] = child\n        \n        # Occasionally apply crossover\n        if crossover_prob > np.random.random():\n            mom1, mom2 = np.random.choice(population, 2, replace=False)\n            crossover(mom1, mom2)\n        \n        # Repair offspring on demand\n        if not all(fit > best_fitness - 0.0001 for fit in evaluate_all(population).values()):\n            most_recent = np.argmax([fitness(s) for s in population])\n            best_solution = population[most_recent]\n            best_fitness = fitness(best_solution)\n            if np.random.random() < 0.2 and crossover_prob > np.random.random():\n                # Sudden crossover boost\n                crossover(best_solution, population[np.random.randint(len(population))])\n        \n        # Stochastic local search for best candidate\n        if np.random.random() < 0.01 and gen % 10 == 0:\n            best_solution = local_search(best_solution)\n            best_fitness = fitness(best_solution)\n        \n        # Time budget check\n        time_spent = time.time() - time_start\n        if time_spent > min(30, time_budget/10):\n            time_remaining -= time_spent\n        \n        # Convergence check\n        if np.allclose([best_fitness] * len(population), [fitness(s) for s in population]):\n            time_remaining = 0\n    \n    # Final local refinement\n    pbar = tqdm(total=time_budget, initial=0)\n    while time_remaining > 10:\n        best_solution = local_search(best_solution)\n        time_remaining -= 10\n        pbar.update(10)\n    \n    pbar.close()\n    return(best_solution, fitness(best_solution), n_points)\n\n4.Search choices and variations\n- Population size: 40 (lower than parent for time efficiency)\n- Mutation probability: 0.05 (higher than parent's 0.1 to promote exploration)\n- Crossover probability: 0.95 (higher for continuous solution representation)\n- Local search probability: 0.01 (conditional on generation number)\n- Roll credits uses statistical adjustment to enforce sum constraint without iterative repair\n- Early termination if the population stabilizes\n\n5.Compute budget and robustness\n- Each candidate evaluation: O(n log n) correlation via FFT (40*time_points^log2(n) operations)\n- Repair: O(1) due to vectorized operations\n- Local search: O(n^2) per local step (limited by time_remaining)\n- Total evaluations: ~40 * 30 * (n_fast + n_medium + n_slow) with descending correlation lengths\n\n6.Validation and return contract\nDone",
